WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:36.250
Start one operation. The gateway starts or receives trace context for checkout. Its span represents one part of the operation, not all system traffic.

2
00:00:36.250 --> 00:01:09.083
Propagate context. Booking receives compatible context and creates a related span. Without propagation, downstream records can appear as unrelated operations.

3
00:01:09.083 --> 00:02:00.167
Record parallel work. Database and payment calls run in parallel in this teaching example. Their intervals overlap, so adding both durations would overstate wall-clock time.

4
00:02:00.167 --> 00:02:39.583
Collect span evidence. Instrumentation exports spans to an observability backend. Export buffering, sampling and failures mean a trace view is not a complete audit of every request.

5
00:02:39.583 --> 00:03:15.000
Find the slow boundary. Compare the critical path, errors and dependency waits. Combine traces with aggregate metrics to determine whether the visible slow request reflects a widespread problem.

6
00:03:15.000 --> 00:04:02.000
Protect telemetry data. Do not attach secrets or unnecessary personal data to spans. Keep attribute cardinality, access, sampling and retention within a deliberate observability budget.
